==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|royaltee}}, {{m|enm|roialtee}}, {{m|enm|royalte}}, from {{der|en|fro|roialté}}, {{m|fro|roiauté}}, {{m|fro|realté}} (compare earlier {{cog|fro|realted|t=realm, kingdom}}), from {{der|en|la-vul|*rēgālitās}}, from {{der|en|la|rēgālis}}, equivalent to {{af|en|royal|-ty}}. {{doublet|en|regality}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɔɪəlti/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-royalty.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|roy|al|ty}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[rank]], [[status]], [[power]] or [[authority]] of a monarch.
# People of [[royal]] [[rank]], plus their [[families]], treated as a [[group]].
# A royal [[right]] or [[prerogative]], such as the [[exploitation]] of a [[natural resource]]; the [[granting]] of such a right; [[payment]] received for such a right.
# The payment received by an owner of real property for exploitation of [[mineral right]]s in the property.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Payment made to a [[writer]], [[composer]], [[inventor]] etc. for the [[sale]] or [[use]] of [[intellectual property]], [[invention]] etc.
# {{lb|en|authorship}} To make more money from a book than it cost to run an advertising campaign for it; to make enough in royalties to cover the [[advance]] a book received.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Someone in a privileged position.
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Daniel Taylor|title=England humiliated as Iceland knock them out of Euro 2016|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/jun/27/england-iceland-euro-2016-match-report|date=27 June 2016|passage=England will have another manager for the next World Cup, Hodgson’s reign will be defined by a result comparable to losing to the United States in the 1950 World Cup and the now-familiar inquest will begin again in a country that likes to see itself as football '''royalty'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261017124100/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/jun/27/england-iceland-euro-2016-match-report|archivedate=17 October 2026}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Theodore Schleifer|title=How Biden Lost George Clooney and Hollywood|date=2024-07-11|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/11/us/politics/biden-george-clooney-hollywood.html|author2=Jacob Bernstein|author3=Reid J. Epstein|passage=The Biden large-donor scene, where Mr. Katzenberg is treated as '''royalty''' himself, has been devastated since Mr. Biden’s debate performance two weeks ago. |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250630122233/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/11/us/politics/biden-george-clooney-hollywood.html|archivedate=30 June 2025}}
# {{lb|en|poker|slang}} A [[king]] and a [[queen]] as a starting hand in [[Texas hold 'em]].
# {{lb|en|Scotland|historical}} The [[bounds]] of a [[royal burgh]].

